  <h1>How Brain Injury Settlements Are Calculated</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Greer Holiday (GH), Editor-in-Chief &mdash; Neurotrauma Practice. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Traumatic brain injury settlements are calculated by combining several damages categories, each with its own valuation logic. This guide walks through the framework that catastrophic injury attorneys, life-care planners, and economists use in practice. It is more detailed than the methodology summary on the home page and includes worked examples for moderate and severe TBI scenarios.</p>

  <h2>The five damages categories</h2>

  <p>A TBI case typically claims damages in five categories:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Past medical expenses.</li>
    <li>Future medical expenses (the life-care plan).</li>
    <li>Lost earnings &mdash; past and future earning capacity.</li>
    <li>Pain and suffering / non-economic damages.</li>
    <li>Loss of consortium and family damages.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Punitive damages may be added in cases of gross negligence or intentional conduct. Each category has its own evidentiary requirements, its own experts, and its own valuation conventions.</p>

  <h2>Past medical expenses</h2>

  <p>The simplest category to document but worth examining carefully. Past medical expenses include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Emergency response and emergency department care.</li>
    <li>Acute hospitalization, which for severe TBI commonly runs $50K&ndash;$500K+ depending on length of stay, ICU days, and surgical intervention.</li>
    <li>Neurosurgical procedures where indicated (hematoma evacuation, decompressive craniectomy, intracranial pressure monitoring).</li>
    <li>Inpatient rehabilitation, typically $50K&ndash;$200K for moderate-to-severe TBI patients who receive intensive inpatient rehab.</li>
    <li>Outpatient therapy: cognitive rehabilitation, speech-language therapy, occupational therapy, physical therapy, vestibular therapy.</li>
    <li>Neuropsychological evaluation, which is essential evidence in nearly every TBI case beyond minor concussion. A complete battery runs $3K&ndash;$8K.</li>
    <li>Medications, including antiseizure medications, antispasticity drugs, mood stabilizers, sleep aids, and stimulants used for cognitive symptoms.</li>
    <li>Equipment and home modifications already incurred.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The legal nuance: in some jurisdictions, past medical expenses are claimed at the amount billed by providers; in others, they are claimed at the amount actually paid (which may be substantially less due to insurance write-offs and contractual adjustments). This is the "collateral source rule" issue and varies materially by state.</p>

  <h2>Future medical expenses: the life-care plan</h2>

  <p>For moderate-to-severe TBI, future medical expenses are typically the largest single damages component. A life-care plan, prepared by a certified life care planner (often working with a physiatrist or rehabilitation specialist), itemizes projected costs year by year through expected lifespan.</p>

  <p>Categories that commonly appear in TBI life-care plans:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Attendant care.</strong> The largest expense for severe TBI with cognitive impairment. Severe TBI patients often require 24-hour supervision &mdash; not necessarily skilled care, but a caregiver who can ensure safety, manage medication, support activities of daily living, and respond to behavioral or seizure events. Cost ranges from $50/hour skilled to $25/hour unskilled, producing annual totals of $150K&ndash;$300K+ for 24-hour coverage. Vegetative or minimally conscious patients may require skilled nursing facility care at $300K&ndash;$500K/year.</li>
    <li><strong>Cognitive rehabilitation.</strong> Ongoing neuropsychological therapy, cognitive retraining, and (for some patients) day-program participation. $30K&ndash;$80K/year ongoing.</li>
    <li><strong>Speech and swallowing therapy.</strong> Significant for patients with aphasia, dysarthria, or dysphagia. $15K&ndash;$40K/year.</li>
    <li><strong>Occupational and physical therapy.</strong> Ongoing to maintain function and address motor deficits. $10K&ndash;$30K/year.</li>
    <li><strong>Medical care and medications.</strong> Ongoing physician visits (neurology, physiatry, psychiatry), specialist consultations, and pharmaceutical management. $10K&ndash;$30K/year.</li>
    <li><strong>Equipment and home modifications.</strong> Communication devices, mobility equipment, hospital beds (for severe patients), accessible bathroom and kitchen modifications, vehicle modifications. $50K&ndash;$200K initial plus recurring replacements every 5&ndash;10 years.</li>
    <li><strong>Hospitalizations and emergencies.</strong> TBI patients have elevated rates of seizure-related admissions, behavioral crises, aspiration pneumonia, and orthopedic injury from falls. Life-care plans typically include a recurring expectancy.</li>
    <li><strong>Case management.</strong> Ongoing care coordination, especially for patients with complex multi-specialty needs.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The life-care plan projects these costs through expected lifespan. Severe TBI generally reduces life expectancy modestly relative to the uninjured population, with greater reductions for vegetative and minimally conscious outcomes. The life-care planner consults actuarial tables and TBI-specific mortality literature.</p>

  <p>An economist then reduces the year-by-year projection to present value using a defensible discount rate (typically a real risk-free rate of 1&ndash;3% annually, varying by jurisdiction and economist preference). The choice of discount rate has substantial impact on the present value, particularly for long projections.</p>

  <p>Present-value totals for future medical care in severe TBI commonly range from $3M (severe TBI with substantial recovery, age 50) to $12M+ (catastrophic TBI requiring 24-hour care, age 30).</p>

  <h2>Lost earnings: past and future earning capacity</h2>

  <p>Lost earnings are calculated as pre-injury earning capacity minus post-injury residual earning capacity, projected through expected work-life expectancy.</p>

  <p>The economist's analysis typically includes:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Past lost wages: documented wages and benefits lost from injury through trial.</li>
    <li>Future lost earning capacity: pre-injury earnings trajectory (extrapolated from individual employment history and Bureau of Labor Statistics data) minus post-injury residual earning capacity. The residual capacity is informed by vocational rehabilitation expert analysis.</li>
    <li>Lost benefits: health insurance, retirement contributions, employer-paid life insurance, and other compensation not appearing in the W-2 wages.</li>
    <li>Household services: the value of services the injured person previously performed (housekeeping, childcare, yard maintenance, home repair) that now need to be hired out. Standard reference is the Dollar Value of Household Services tables.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>TBI cases pose distinctive vocational challenges. Cognitive impairment is the central impact, and its vocational consequences depend heavily on pre-injury occupation. A construction laborer with a moderate TBI may regain enough function to return to similar physical work. A software engineer or attorney with the same injury may lose access to nearly all of their pre-injury income stream because their work requires the precise cognitive functions that TBI tends to impair: working memory, executive function, attention, processing speed, and complex problem-solving.</p>

  <p>A vocational rehabilitation expert evaluates: what jobs can the patient realistically perform given the documented cognitive profile, what wages do those jobs pay, and what is the loss relative to the pre-injury trajectory? For a high-income knowledge worker with moderate-to-severe TBI, the lost-earnings present value can be $2M&ndash;$5M+.</p>

  <h2>Pain and suffering / non-economic damages</h2>

  <p>Non-economic damages compensate for the injury itself, ongoing pain, mood disturbance, loss of identity, loss of life enjoyment, and the cognitive and behavioral impact of the injury on daily life. For TBI specifically, the qualitative impact often dominates: a person who used to be able to read, problem-solve, and engage socially at a high level may lose those abilities even when their physical function is preserved.</p>

  <p>Non-economic damages are typically calculated by one of two methods:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Multiplier method.</strong> Economic damages multiplied by a factor reflecting injury severity. Multipliers range from 1.5x for minor injuries through 5x or higher for catastrophic injuries. TBI cases commonly use multipliers in the 2x&ndash;4x range, with higher multipliers for cases with substantial cognitive or behavioral impact.</li>
    <li><strong>Per-diem method.</strong> A daily value (often anchored to daily earnings or a defensible daily-suffering rate) multiplied by the expected number of days of suffering. Less common in TBI cases.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Most states have no statutory cap on non-economic damages in non-medical-malpractice catastrophic injury cases. Medical malpractice caps apply in some states; readers in those states should consult the specific statutory regime.</p>

  <h2>Loss of consortium and family damages</h2>

  <p>Loss of consortium is the spouse's claim for loss of the injured person's society, companionship, services, and (in some states) sexual relationship. Some states extend consortium-style claims to children (for loss of parental consortium) and parents (for loss of filial consortium).</p>

  <p>TBI creates particularly severe consortium impact. Unlike orthopedic injuries that leave the personality intact, TBI often changes the injured person's personality, judgment, emotional regulation, and social functioning. The spouse describes losing the person they married, even though that person is still physically present. Loss of consortium claims in serious TBI cases commonly add $500K&ndash;$5M+ to the recovery.</p>

  <h2>Jurisdictional adjustments</h2>

  <p>Some jurisdictions are notably favorable for catastrophic injury plaintiffs:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>California, New York, Pennsylvania, Illinois (Cook County), New Jersey:</strong> well-developed catastrophic injury practice, no general damages caps, plaintiff-friendly juries. Median recoveries often 10&ndash;20% above national average.</li>
    <li><strong>Florida, Massachusetts, Maryland, Connecticut, Washington:</strong> standard plaintiff jurisdictions; recoveries near national average.</li>
    <li><strong>Texas, Georgia, and several Southern and Mountain West states:</strong> generally less plaintiff-friendly, with various damage caps (particularly in medical malpractice) and conservative juries. Recoveries often below national average for similar cases.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Defendant insurance and assets</h2>

  <p>Settlement is constrained by what the defendant can pay. Even a $20M nominal damages claim settles for far less if available insurance is $1M and the defendant has no significant additional assets. An experienced catastrophic injury attorney maps available coverage early and often pursues multiple defendants and coverage layers (commercial auto, umbrella, dram shop, premises liability, manufacturer defect) to maximize recovery.</p>

  <h2>Worked example: moderate TBI</h2>

  <p>35-year-old software engineer with moderate TBI (GCS 11 at admission, focal contusion plus diffuse axonal injury on MRI) from a motor vehicle accident in California. Pre-injury income $120K. Documented cognitive deficits in working memory and processing speed; able to return to work part-time at reduced responsibility level earning $50K.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Past medical expenses: $250K.</li>
    <li>Future medical care (ongoing cognitive rehab, periodic neuropsychological re-evaluation, medication management, present value): $750K.</li>
    <li>Lost earnings (present value of $70K/year differential through work-life expectancy): $2.1M.</li>
    <li>Pain and suffering (3x multiplier on economic damages): $9.3M.</li>
    <li>Loss of consortium for spouse: $750K.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Total nominal damages claim: ~$13.2M. Practical settlement depends on insurance; with $1M auto policy plus $5M umbrella, typical settlement might be $4M&ndash;$6M.</p>

  <h2>Worked example: severe TBI</h2>

  <p>28-year-old with severe TBI (GCS 6 at admission, diffuse axonal injury with brainstem involvement) from a commercial truck collision in California. Pre-injury income $55K. Plateau at Rancho VI; requires 24-hour supervision but ambulates and has limited communication.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Past medical expenses: $1.1M.</li>
    <li>Future medical care (24-hour attendant care, ongoing rehab, equipment, present value): $8.5M.</li>
    <li>Lost earnings (unable to return to competitive employment; present value): $2.5M.</li>
    <li>Pain and suffering (3x multiplier on economic damages): $36M.</li>
    <li>Loss of consortium for spouse: $1.5M.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Total nominal damages claim: ~$50M. Commercial truck policy stack often supports $5M&ndash;$15M settlement; if additional defendants (loading shipper, vehicle manufacturer) are in the case, recovery can be substantially higher.</p>

  <h2>What this guide does not cover</h2>

  <p>This guide focuses on the damages framework. It does not address: liability theory and proof, multi-defendant apportionment, comparative negligence defenses, the specifics of bringing claims under particular state statutes, settlement negotiation strategy, structured settlement design, or the interaction with workers' compensation and government benefits. Those topics are case-specific and require an experienced catastrophic injury attorney with neurotrauma experience.</p>

